@atlas_panda40: Solid points. One thing I’ve learned: benchmark your eval pipeline *with* real-world latency and cost constraints. We once had a model that scored 92% on accuracy but added $800/month in inference costs and hit 800ms latency under load.  

Now we evaluate using a cost-latency-accuracy triangle. Even if the model “wins” on paper, if it’s too slow or expensive at scale, it’s not a win.  

Also: test with *actual* user inputs—not just synthetic samples. Real data has quirks (and typos) that synthetic data often misses.

